{"black_box_warning":{"current_value":"No FDA boxed warning identified in selected current DailyMed label.","fda_source_urls":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2024/008372s074lbl.pdf"],"proposed_value":"No FDA boxed warning identified in FDA Acthar Gel prescribing information.","rationale":"The no-boxed-warning conclusion is supported by the FDA accessdata label for Acthar Gel, which contains warnings and precautions but no boxed-warning section or boxed-warning text. The current row wording and source rely on DailyMed, which is not permissible for this field.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"incorrect"},"blocking_questions":[],"fact_checks":[{"current_value":"Infectious: infection 20%, neurologic: convulsion 12%, vascular/cardiac: hypertension 11%, behavioral: irritability 7%, constitutional: pyrexia 5%, cardiac: cardiac hypertrophy 3%, endocrine: Cushingoid 3%, GI: diarrhea 3%, GI: vomiting 3%, GI: decreased appetite 3% (FDA infantile-spasms data at recommended 75 U/m2 BID dose).","field":"adverse_symptoms_percentages","proposed_value":"Infectious: infection 20%, neurologic: convulsion 12%, vascular/cardiac: hypertension 11%, behavioral: irritability 7%, constitutional: pyrexia 5%, cardiac: cardiac hypertrophy 3%, endocrine: Cushingoid 3%, GI: diarrhea 3%, GI: vomiting 3%, GI: decreased appetite 3% (FDA infantile-spasms data at recommended 75 U/m2 BID dose).","rationale":"FDA labeling tabulates these adverse-reaction percentages for infants and children under 2 years treated with Acthar Gel at the recommended 75 U/m2 twice-daily regimen.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2024/008372s074lbl.pdf"]},{"current_value":"adrenocorticotropic hormone; ACTH; corticotropin","field":"alternate_generic_names","proposed_value":"adrenocorticotropic hormone; ACTH; corticotropin","rationale":"FDA orphan-drug record lists repository corticotropin or adrenocorticotropic hormone, and NCBI MeSH lists ACTH and corticotropin as entry terms for adrenocorticotropic hormone.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/scripts/opdlisting/oopd/detailedIndex.cfm?cfgridkey=168103","https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/sites/entrez?Cmd=DetailsSearch&Db=mesh&Term=%22Adrenocorticotropic+Hormone%22%5BMeSH+Terms%5D"]},{"current_value":"Yes","field":"available_in_us","proposed_value":"Yes","rationale":"Acthar Gel has FDA labeling and an FDA orphan marketing approval record for infantile spasms, supporting US availability.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2024/008372s074lbl.pdf","https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/scripts/opdlisting/oopd/detailedIndex.cfm?cfgridkey=168103"]},{"current_value":"Not a CYP inducer/inhibitor; FDA label notes steroidogenic/electrolyte effects and that Acthar Gel may accentuate electrolyte loss associated with diuretics.","field":"enzyme_inducing_or_inhibiting","proposed_value":"Formal drug-drug interaction studies have not been performed; FDA label states Acthar Gel may accentuate the electrolyte loss associated with diuretic therapy. No FDA-label CYP induction/inhibition interaction was identified.","rationale":"The FDA label supports the diuretic/electrolyte interaction and states formal drug-drug interaction studies have not been performed. I did not find FDA-label support for the stronger statement that it is not a CYP inducer/inhibitor.","requires_user_approval":true,"status":"missing_source","supporting_sources":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2024/008372s074lbl.pdf"]},{"current_value":"Epileptic spasms / infantile spasms","field":"epilepsy_type","proposed_value":"Epileptic spasms / infantile spasms","rationale":"FDA labeling indicates Acthar Gel as monotherapy for infantile spasms in infants and children under 2 years of age; FDA orphan record uses the same infantile-spasms indication.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2024/008372s074lbl.pdf","https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/scripts/opdlisting/oopd/detailedIndex.cfm?cfgridkey=168103"]},{"current_value":"FDA/DailyMed Acthar Gel label (https://dailymed.nlm.nih.gov/dailymed/drugInfo.cfm?setid=7b48ddec-e815-45f4-9ca0-5c0daaf56f30); FDA orphan drug designation/approval record for repository corticotropin/adrenocorticotropic hormone (https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/scripts/opdlisting/oopd/detailedIndex.cfm?cfgridkey=168103); FDA/DailyMed labeling","field":"evidence_sources","proposed_value":"FDA Acthar Gel label, NDA 008372/S-074, revised 02/2024 (https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2024/008372s074lbl.pdf); FDA orphan drug designation/approval record for repository corticotropin/adrenocorticotropic hormone, H.P. Acthar Gel, infantile spasms marketing approval 10/15/2010 (https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/scripts/opdlisting/oopd/detailedIndex.cfm?cfgridkey=168103); NCBI MeSH Adrenocorticotropic Hormone entry terms for ACTH/corticotropin aliases (https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/sites/entrez?Cmd=DetailsSearch&Db=mesh&Term=%22Adrenocorticotropic+Hormone%22%5BMeSH+Terms%5D); Tran et al. 2022 scoping review, PMID 34902005, for corticotropin RCT context (https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/34902005/).","rationale":"The current source field relies on DailyMed and duplicates FDA/DailyMed labeling. Replace with trusted FDA/NCBI/PubMed sources that support the row facts and RCT context.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"incorrect","supporting_sources":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2024/008372s074lbl.pdf","https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/scripts/opdlisting/oopd/detailedIndex.cfm?cfgridkey=168103","https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/sites/entrez?Cmd=DetailsSearch&Db=mesh&Term=%22Adrenocorticotropic+Hormone%22%5BMeSH+Terms%5D","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/34902005/"]},{"current_value":"Repository corticotropin injectable gel; multi-dose vial for intramuscular use in infantile spasms; prefilled subcutaneous injector for adult non-seizure indications","field":"formulations_available","proposed_value":"Repository corticotropin injectable gel; multi-dose vial for intramuscular use in infantile spasms; prefilled subcutaneous injector for adult non-seizure indications","rationale":"FDA labeling lists the multi-dose vial and 40-unit/80-unit single-dose pre-filled SelfJect injectors; it specifies infantile-spasms dosing must use the vial intramuscularly and that the SelfJect injector is for adults only.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2024/008372s074lbl.pdf"]},{"current_value":"ACTH plasma half-life about 15 min after IV administration; pharmacokinetics of Acthar Gel not adequately characterized","field":"half_life_range","proposed_value":"ACTH plasma half-life about 15 min after IV administration; pharmacokinetics of Acthar Gel not adequately characterized","rationale":"FDA labeling states ACTH disappears rapidly after IV administration, plasma half-life is about 15 minutes, and Acthar Gel pharmacokinetics have not been adequately characterized.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2024/008372s074lbl.pdf"]},{"current_value":"Limited/unknown; peptide hormone mixture with adrenal cortical target effects rather than a conventional hepatic/renal ASM metabolism profile","field":"major_organ_for_metabolism","proposed_value":"Limited/unknown; FDA label states pharmacokinetics of Acthar Gel have not been adequately characterized and describes Acthar Gel as a naturally sourced complex mixture of adrenocorticotropic hormone analogs and other pituitary peptides with adrenal cortical effects.","rationale":"The label supports limited/unknown pharmacokinetics and adrenal cortical effects. The comparison to a conventional hepatic/renal ASM metabolism profile is an inference not directly sourced in the row.","requires_user_approval":true,"status":"missing_source","supporting_sources":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2024/008372s074lbl.pdf"]},{"current_value":"Infantile spasms: 150 U/m2/day divided BID during the 2-week treatment course, followed by taper.","field":"maximum_approved_daily_dose","proposed_value":"Infantile spasms: 150 U/m2/day divided BID during the 2-week treatment course, followed by taper.","rationale":"FDA labeling gives 150 U/m2 daily, divided into twice-daily IM injections of 75 U/m2 for 2 weeks, then taper over 2 weeks.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2024/008372s074lbl.pdf"]},{"current_value":"Moderate","field":"mechanism_confidence","proposed_value":"Moderate","rationale":"Moderate confidence is consistent with FDA labeling: the infantile-spasms mechanism is unknown, but adrenal cortical stimulation and melanocortin-receptor binding are described.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2024/008372s074lbl.pdf"]},{"current_value":"FDA labeling states the mechanism of action in infantile spasms is unknown; Acthar Gel and endogenous ACTH stimulate adrenal cortical secretion of cortisol, corticosterone, aldosterone, and weak androgenic substances, and Acthar Gel is also reported to bind melanocortin receptors.","field":"mechanism_of_action","proposed_value":"FDA labeling states the mechanism of action in infantile spasms is unknown; Acthar Gel and endogenous ACTH stimulate adrenal cortical secretion of cortisol, corticosterone, aldosterone, and weak androgenic substances, and Acthar Gel is also reported to bind melanocortin receptors.","rationale":"This matches FDA label section 12.1.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2024/008372s074lbl.pdf"]},{"current_value":"FDA/DailyMed Acthar Gel label (https://dailymed.nlm.nih.gov/dailymed/drugInfo.cfm?setid=7b48ddec-e815-45f4-9ca0-5c0daaf56f30)","field":"mechanism_source","proposed_value":"FDA Acthar Gel label, NDA 008372/S-074, section 12.1 Mechanism of Action (https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2024/008372s074lbl.pdf)","rationale":"DailyMed should be replaced with the FDA accessdata label source.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"incorrect","supporting_sources":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2024/008372s074lbl.pdf"]},{"current_value":"FDA label","field":"mechanism_source_tier","proposed_value":"FDA label","rationale":"The proposed mechanism source is an FDA accessdata label, so the source tier remains correct.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2024/008372s074lbl.pdf"]},{"current_value":"FDA-labeled infantile-spasms regimen: 150 U/m2/day divided BID for 2 weeks before taper; supportive trial studied lower-dose regimens but labeled dosing uses 150 U/m2/day.","field":"minimum_effective_dose","proposed_value":"FDA-labeled infantile-spasms regimen: 150 U/m2/day divided BID for 2 weeks before taper; supportive trial studied lower-dose regimens but labeled dosing uses 150 U/m2/day.","rationale":"FDA labeling gives this regimen and describes a supportive high-dose versus low-dose trial outside the placebo-controlled RCT section.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2024/008372s074lbl.pdf"]},{"current_value":"N/A","field":"pubmed_phase_ii_iii_rct_links","proposed_value":"N/A","rationale":"No qualifying phase II/III placebo-controlled epilepsy RCT link is present. FDA-labeled infantile-spasms evidence consists of an ACTH-vs-prednisone randomized study and a high-dose versus low-dose supportive trial, not a placebo-only controlled epilepsy RCT. A placebo-controlled RCI RCT exists for multiple sclerosis relapse, but that is not an epilepsy/seizure indication.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2024/008372s074lbl.pdf","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/34902005/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/34984839/"]},{"current_value":"ACTH; adrenocorticotropic hormone; corticotropin; Acthar Gel; H.P. Acthar Gel","field":"pubmed_search_aliases","proposed_value":"ACTH; adrenocorticotropic hormone; corticotropin; Acthar Gel; H.P. Acthar Gel","rationale":"Aliases are supported by NCBI MeSH and FDA label/orphan records. They should not be separate drug rows.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/sites/entrez?Cmd=DetailsSearch&Db=mesh&Term=%22Adrenocorticotropic+Hormone%22%5BMeSH+Terms%5D","https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2024/008372s074lbl.pdf","https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/scripts/opdlisting/oopd/detailedIndex.cfm?cfgridkey=168103"]},{"current_value":"No QT interval effect described in FDA labeling; monitor for hypertension, salt/water retention, hypokalemia, and cardiac hypertrophy in infantile-spasms safety data.","field":"qt_interval_effect","proposed_value":"No QT interval effect described in FDA labeling; monitor for hypertension, salt/water retention, hypokalemia, and cardiac hypertrophy in infantile-spasms safety data.","rationale":"FDA labeling emphasizes blood pressure, salt/water retention, hypokalemia, and cardiac hypertrophy in the safety data; no QT warning was identified in the FDA label reviewed.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2024/008372s074lbl.pdf"]},{"current_value":"Acthar Gel; H.P. Acthar Gel","field":"trade_names","proposed_value":"Acthar Gel; H.P. Acthar Gel","rationale":"FDA labeling uses Acthar Gel and FDA orphan record lists H.P. Acthar Gel.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2024/008372s074lbl.pdf","https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/scripts/opdlisting/oopd/detailedIndex.cfm?cfgridkey=168103"]},{"current_value":"Infantile spasms: 150 U/m2/day divided as 75 U/m2 IM twice daily for 2 weeks, then gradual taper over 2 weeks per FDA label.","field":"typical_doses_per_day","proposed_value":"Infantile spasms: 150 U/m2/day divided as 75 U/m2 IM twice daily for 2 weeks, then gradual taper over 2 weeks per FDA label.","rationale":"FDA labeling gives the same infantile-spasms dosing and taper schedule.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2024/008372s074lbl.pdf"]},{"current_value":"1952 initial U.S. approval; FDA orphan marketing approval for infantile spasms 10/15/2010.","field":"year_fda_cleared","proposed_value":"1952 initial U.S. approval; FDA orphan marketing approval for infantile spasms 10/15/2010.","rationale":"FDA label states Initial U.S. Approval: 1952; FDA orphan record lists marketing approval for infantile spasms on 10/15/2010.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2024/008372s074lbl.pdf","https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/scripts/opdlisting/oopd/detailedIndex.cfm?cfgridkey=168103"]}],"generic_name":"repository corticotropin","missing_rcts":[],"outcome_checks":[{"current_value":"N/A","field":"diff_50_responder_maximum_effective_dose","proposed_value":"N/A","rationale":"No qualifying placebo-controlled epilepsy RCT with an RR50 endpoint was identified for repository corticotropin/ACTH in infantile spasms. FDA-labeled infantile-spasms trials use active comparator or 